# GitHub is the only sync target

User directive (Oct 2026): **do not sync or copy the project anywhere else — only GitHub.**

## Agents must NOT

- Run `rsync`, `scp`, `cp -R`, or similar to mirror the repo (M4 Mini, MSD card, NAS, Google Drive, second local folder, etc.).
- Restore or merge from `/Volumes/256GB MSD/Hunch` or other backup volumes unless the user explicitly asks in that message.
- Suggest or run two-way folder sync (Syncthing, Tailscale file copy) as part of finishing a task.
- Create duplicate working trees “for safety” on the same machine.

## Agents MAY

- Edit and run code in the **single** workspace: `/Users/nhharshan/Hunch`.
- Use **git** (`add`, `commit`, `push`, `pull`, `fetch`) against the configured GitHub remotes when the user asks to sync or publish.
- Read GitHub (`origin`, PRs, branches) to determine remote state when the user asks.

## Dual GitHub remotes (required)

Local monorepo publishes to **two** GitHub repos (same `main` content):

| Remote | Repository |
|--------|------------|
| `origin` | `harshannh62212-glitch/HUNCH` |
| `chat-app` | `harshannh62212-glitch/chat-app` |

When the user asks to **sync**, **back up**, or **push** to GitHub, push **both** after any commit:

```bash
./scripts/push-github-both.sh
```

Equivalent: `git push origin main && git push chat-app main`.

If `chat-app` rejects because histories diverged and the user wants the local tree to win, use `git push chat-app main --force-with-lease` once, then use the script for routine pushes.

## Commits and push

- Only commit or push when the user explicitly requests it (unchanged).
- When the user says “sync” or “back up the project,” mean **push to both GitHub remotes** (or pull from GitHub on another clone), not file copy.

## Supersedes

- Older rules that required M4 `rsync` or treated local mirrors as source of truth are **inactive** for agents; this rule wins.
